---
title: Data Guide
---
NIFT_TANSTACK_DOCUMENT_V1
{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Tables start with your data. Your column definitions and rows will depend on the shape of your data. TanStack Table has some TypeScript features that will help you create the rest of your table code with a great type-safe experience. If you set up your data and types correctly, TanStack Table will be able to infer the shape of your data and enforce that your column definitions are made correctly."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"typescript","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TypeScript"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TypeScript is NOT required to use the TanStack Table packages... "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"BUT"}]}]},{"type":"text","value":" TanStack Table is written and organized in such a way that makes the awesome TypeScript experience that you get feel like it is one of the main selling points of the library. If you are not using TypeScript, you will be missing out on a lot of great autocompletion and type-checking features that will both speed up your development time and reduce the number of bugs in your code."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"typescript-generics","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TypeScript Generics"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Having a basic understanding of what TypeScript Generics are and how they work will help you understand this guide better, but it should be easy enough to pick up as you go. The official "},{"type":"link","href":"https://www.typescriptlang.org/docs/handbook/2/generics.html","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TypeScript Generics Docs"}]},{"type":"text","value":" may be helpful for those not yet familiar with TypeScript."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"defining-data-types","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Defining Data Types"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"data"},{"type":"text","value":" is an array of objects that will be turned into the rows of your table. Each object in the array represents a row of data (under normal circumstances). If you are using TypeScript, we usually define a type for the shape of our data. This type is used as a generic type for all of the other table, column, row, and cell instances. This generic is usually referred to as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TData"},{"type":"text","value":" throughout the rest of the TanStack Table types and APIs."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For example, if we have a table that displays a list of users in an array like this:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"[\n  {\n    \"firstName\": \"Tanner\",\n    \"lastName\": \"Linsley\",\n    \"age\": 33,\n    \"visits\": 100,\n    \"progress\": 50,\n    \"status\": \"Married\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"firstName\": \"Kevin\",\n    \"lastName\": \"Vandy\",\n    \"age\": 27,\n    \"visits\": 200,\n    \"progress\": 100,\n    \"status\": \"Single\"\n  }\n]","lang":"json"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Then we can define a User (TData) type like this:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"//TData\ntype User = {\n  firstName: string\n  lastName: string\n  age: number\n  visits: number\n  progress: number\n  status: string\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"We can then define our "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"data"},{"type":"text","value":" array with this type, and then TanStack Table will be able to intelligently infer lots of types for us later on in our columns, rows, cells, etc. This is because the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"data"},{"type":"text","value":" type is literally defined as the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TData"},{"type":"text","value":" generic type. Whatever you pass to the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"data"},{"type":"text","value":" table option will become the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TData"},{"type":"text","value":" type for the rest of the table instance. Just make sure your column definitions use the same "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TData"},{"type":"text","value":" type as the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"data"},{"type":"text","value":" type when you define them later."}]},{"type":"code","value":"//note: data needs a \"stable\" reference to prevent wasteful re-computation (more on this below)\nconst data: Array<User> = []\n//or\nconst [data, setData] = React.useState<Array<User>>([])\n//or\nconst data = ref<Array<User>>([]) //vue\n//etc...","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"deep-keyed-data","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Deep Keyed Data"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If your data is not a nice flat array of objects, that's okay! Once you get around to defining your columns, there are strategies for accessing deeply nested data in your accessors."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If your "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"data"},{"type":"text","value":" looks something like this:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"[\n  {\n    \"name\": {\n      \"first\": \"Tanner\",\n      \"last\": \"Linsley\"\n    },\n    \"info\": {\n      \"age\": 33,\n      \"visits\": 100\n    }\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": {\n      \"first\": \"Kevin\",\n      \"last\": \"Vandy\"\n    },\n    \"info\": {\n      \"age\": 27,\n      \"visits\": 200\n    }\n  }\n]","lang":"json"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You can define a type like this:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"type User = {\n  name: {\n    first: string\n    last: string\n  }\n  info: {\n    age: number\n    visits: number\n  }\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"And you will be able to access the data in your column definitions with either dot notation in an accessorKey or simply by using an accessorFn."}]},{"type":"code","value":"const columns = [\n  {\n    header: 'First Name',\n    accessorKey: 'name.first',\n  },\n  {\n    header: 'Last Name',\n    accessorKey: 'name.last',\n  },\n  {\n    header: 'Age',\n    accessorFn: (row) => row.info.age, //accessorFn receives the whole row object\n  },\n  //...\n]","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This is discussed in more detail in the "},{"type":"link","href":"./column-defs","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Column Def Guide"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"callout","kind":"note","title":"Note","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The \"keys\" in your JSON data can usually be anything, but any periods in an "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"accessorKey"},{"type":"text","value":" will be interpreted as a deep key path. If a key in your data contains a literal period, use an "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"accessorFn"},{"type":"text","value":" to read it instead."}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"nested-sub-row-data","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Nested Sub-Row Data"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If you are using expanding features, it can be common to have nested sub-rows in your data. This results in a recursive type that is a bit different."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"So if your data looks like this:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"[\n  {\n    \"firstName\": \"Tanner\",\n    \"lastName\": \"Linsley\",\n    \"subRows\": [\n      {\n        \"firstName\": \"Kevin\",\n        \"lastName\": \"Vandy\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"firstName\": \"John\",\n        \"lastName\": \"Doe\",\n        \"subRows\": []\n      }\n    ]\n  },\n  {\n    \"firstName\": \"Jane\",\n    \"lastName\": \"Doe\"\n  }\n]","lang":"json"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You can define a type like this:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"type User = {\n  firstName: string\n  lastName: string\n  subRows?: Array<User> //does not have to be called \"subRows\", can be called anything\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Where "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"subRows"},{"type":"text","value":" is an optional array of "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"User"},{"type":"text","value":" objects. This is discussed in more detail in the "},{"type":"link","href":"../framework/react/guide/expanding","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Expanding Guide"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"data-of-unknown-shape","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Data of Unknown Shape"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Sometimes you cannot write a row type at all because the shape of the data is not known ahead of time (arbitrary API responses, user-uploaded CSV files, user-configurable reports). In that case, type your rows as a generic record:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"type DynamicRow = Record<string, unknown>\n\nconst data: Array<DynamicRow> = await fetchWhoKnowsWhat()","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You give up per-column value inference, but everything else still works. Column definitions are then generated from the data itself at runtime instead of being hard-coded. See "},{"type":"link","href":"./column-defs#dynamic-column-definitions","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Dynamic Column Definitions"}]},{"type":"text","value":" in the Column Definitions Guide and the "},{"type":"link","href":"../framework/react/examples/basic-dynamic-columns","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Dynamic Columns example"}]},{"type":"text","value":" (available for every framework adapter) for the full pattern, including runtime data type detection for sorting and filtering."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"give-data-a-stable-reference","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Give Data a \"Stable\" Reference"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"data"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"columns"},{"type":"text","value":" arrays that you pass to the table instance should have \"stable\" references to prevent wasteful re-computation and rendering bugs."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"In v9, table state lives in TanStack Store atoms, and the table instance is only created once. Passing a new "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"data"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"columns"},{"type":"text","value":" reference on every render will not, by itself, throw the table into the classic v8-style infinite re-render loop. However, the row models and column structures are memoized against the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"data"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"columns"},{"type":"text","value":" references, so an unstable reference still has real consequences:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Wasted re-computation."}]},{"type":"text","value":" A new "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"data"},{"type":"text","value":" reference invalidates the core row model, so every row and cell object is rebuilt from scratch on every render (and any sorted, filtered, grouped, or paginated row models recompute right along with it). A new "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"columns"},{"type":"text","value":" reference does the same for the column and header structures. For large datasets, this is a serious performance problem."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Auto-reset render loops."}]},{"type":"text","value":" Features that automatically reset state when the rows are recalculated can still cause infinite re-render loops. For example, with client-side pagination enabled, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"autoResetPageIndex"},{"type":"text","value":" resets the page index whenever the row model recomputes. With an unstable "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"data"},{"type":"text","value":" reference, that means a state update after every render, and each state update triggers another render with yet another new "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"data"},{"type":"text","value":" reference."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Either way, treat stable "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"data"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"columns"},{"type":"text","value":" references as a requirement."}]},{"type":"callout","kind":"note","title":"Note","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When React Compiler successfully compiles a component, it can provide stable identities for values and calculations automatically. TanStack Table's examples keep the explicit patterns below so they also work without the compiler. See the "},{"type":"link","href":"../framework/react/guide/react-compiler","children":[{"type":"text","value":"React Compiler Guide"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for when manual memoization can be removed."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"How you do this depends on which framework adapter you are using, but in React, you should often use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"React.useState"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"React.useMemo"},{"type":"text","value":", or similar to ensure that both the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"data"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"columns"},{"type":"text","value":" table options have stable references."}]},{"type":"code","value":"const fallbackData: Array<User> = [] //✅ GOOD: stable empty fallback for when your data can be undefined (e.g. while a query loads)\nconst features = tableFeatures({}) //✅ GOOD: define outside the component for a stable reference\n\nexport default function MyComponent() {\n  //✅ GOOD: `columns` is a stable reference\n  const columns = useMemo(\n    () => [\n      // ...\n    ],\n    [],\n  )\n\n  //✅ GOOD: `data` is a stable reference\n  const [data, setData] = useState<Array<User>>(() => [\n    // ...\n  ])\n\n  // Columns and data have stable references, so the table only recomputes when they actually change\n  const table = useTable({\n    features,\n    columns,\n    data, // from useState, so always defined; use `data ?? fallbackData` when your data can be undefined\n  })\n\n  return <table>...</table>\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"React.useState"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"React.useMemo"},{"type":"text","value":" are not the only ways to give your data a stable reference. You can also define your data outside of the component or use a 3rd party state management library like Redux, Zustand, or TanStack Query."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The main thing to avoid is defining the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"data"},{"type":"text","value":" array inside the same scope as the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useTable"},{"type":"text","value":" call. That will cause the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"data"},{"type":"text","value":" array to be redefined on every render, which forces the table to rebuild every row on every render (and can loop forever when auto-reset features are involved)."}]},{"type":"code","value":"export default function MyComponent() {\n  //❌ BAD: `features` is re-created on every render and re-applied to the table on every render\n  const features = tableFeatures({})\n\n  //😵 BAD: `columns` is redefined as a new array on every render, so the column and header structures are rebuilt on every render!\n  const columns = [\n    // ...\n  ]\n\n  //😵 BAD: `data` is redefined as a new array on every render, so every row is rebuilt on every render!\n  const data = [\n    // ...\n  ]\n\n  //❌ Columns and data are defined in the same scope as `useTable` without stable references\n  const table = useTable({\n    features,\n    columns,\n    data: data ?? [], //❌ Also bad because the fallback array is re-created on every render\n  })\n\n  return <table>...</table>\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"memoize-data-transformations","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Memoize Data Transformations"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Even if your source data already has a stable reference, transforming it inline destroys that stability. Something as simple as an inline "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"data.filter()"},{"type":"text","value":" creates a brand new array on every render."}]},{"type":"code","value":"export default function MyComponent() {\n  //✅ GOOD (TanStack Query provides stable references to data automatically)\n  const { data, isLoading } = useQuery({\n    //...\n  })\n\n  const table = useTable({\n    features,\n    columns,\n    //❌ BAD: This creates a new array on every render (destroys the stable reference)\n    data: data?.filter((d) => d.isActive) ?? fallbackData,\n  })\n\n  return <table>...</table>\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Instead, always memoize your data transformations. In React, this can be done with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useMemo"},{"type":"text","value":" or similar."}]},{"type":"code","value":"export default function MyComponent() {\n  //✅ GOOD\n  const { data, isLoading } = useQuery({\n    //...\n  })\n\n  //✅ GOOD: `filteredData` only gets a new reference when `data` changes\n  const filteredData = useMemo(\n    () => data?.filter((d) => d.isActive) ?? [],\n    [data],\n  )\n\n  const table = useTable({\n    features,\n    columns,\n    data: filteredData, // stable reference!\n  })\n\n  return <table>...</table>\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"See the "},{"type":"link","href":"../framework/react/examples/with-tanstack-query","children":[{"type":"text","value":"With TanStack Query example"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for a complete integration with fetched data."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"how-tanstack-table-transforms-data","children":[{"type":"text","value":"How TanStack Table Transforms Data"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Later, in other parts of these docs, you will see how TanStack Table processes the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"data"},{"type":"text","value":" that you pass to the table and generates the row and cell objects that are used to create the table. The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"data"},{"type":"text","value":" that you pass to the table is never mutated by TanStack Table, but the actual values in the rows and cells may be transformed by the accessors in your column definitions, or by other transformations performed by "},{"type":"link","href":"./row-models","children":[{"type":"text","value":"row models"}]},{"type":"text","value":" like grouping or aggregation."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"how-much-data-can-tanstack-table-handle","children":[{"type":"text","value":"How Much Data Can TanStack Table Handle?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack Table's client-side row models are built to handle large datasets, and the official examples include much larger stress tests. The practical limit depends on the payload, table features, rendering strategy, and target hardware, so test with representative data instead of choosing from row count alone."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"See the "},{"type":"link","href":"./client-side-vs-server-side","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Client-Side vs Server-Side Guide"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for the complete decision framework and the distinction between data processing, pagination, and virtualization."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"typescript","text":"TypeScript","level":2},{"id":"typescript-generics","text":"TypeScript Generics","level":3},{"id":"defining-data-types","text":"Defining Data Types","level":3},{"id":"deep-keyed-data","text":"Deep Keyed Data","level":3},{"id":"nested-sub-row-data","text":"Nested Sub-Row Data","level":3},{"id":"data-of-unknown-shape","text":"Data of Unknown Shape","level":3},{"id":"give-data-a-stable-reference","text":"Give Data a \"Stable\" Reference","level":2},{"id":"memoize-data-transformations","text":"Memoize Data Transformations","level":3},{"id":"how-tanstack-table-transforms-data","text":"How TanStack Table Transforms Data","level":2},{"id":"how-much-data-can-tanstack-table-handle","text":"How Much Data Can TanStack Table Handle?","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nTables start with your data. Your column definitions and rows will depend on the shape of your data. TanStack Table has some TypeScript features that will help you create the rest of your table code with a great type-safe experience. If you set up your data and types correctly, TanStack Table will be able to infer the shape of your data and enforce that your column definitions are made correctly.\n\n## TypeScript\n\nTypeScript is NOT required to use the TanStack Table packages... **_BUT_** TanStack Table is written and organized in such a way that makes the awesome TypeScript experience that you get feel like it is one of the main selling points of the library. If you are not using TypeScript, you will be missing out on a lot of great autocompletion and type-checking features that will both speed up your development time and reduce the number of bugs in your code.\n\n### TypeScript Generics\n\nHaving a basic understanding of what TypeScript Generics are and how they work will help you understand this guide better, but it should be easy enough to pick up as you go. The official [TypeScript Generics Docs](https://www.typescriptlang.org/docs/handbook/2/generics.html) may be helpful for those not yet familiar with TypeScript.\n\n### Defining Data Types\n\n`data` is an array of objects that will be turned into the rows of your table. Each object in the array represents a row of data (under normal circumstances). If you are using TypeScript, we usually define a type for the shape of our data. This type is used as a generic type for all of the other table, column, row, and cell instances. This generic is usually referred to as `TData` throughout the rest of the TanStack Table types and APIs.\n\nFor example, if we have a table that displays a list of users in an array like this:\n\n```json\n[\n  {\n    \"firstName\": \"Tanner\",\n    \"lastName\": \"Linsley\",\n    \"age\": 33,\n    \"visits\": 100,\n    \"progress\": 50,\n    \"status\": \"Married\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"firstName\": \"Kevin\",\n    \"lastName\": \"Vandy\",\n    \"age\": 27,\n    \"visits\": 200,\n    \"progress\": 100,\n    \"status\": \"Single\"\n  }\n]\n```\n\nThen we can define a User (TData) type like this:\n\n```ts\n//TData\ntype User = {\n  firstName: string\n  lastName: string\n  age: number\n  visits: number\n  progress: number\n  status: string\n}\n```\n\nWe can then define our `data` array with this type, and then TanStack Table will be able to intelligently infer lots of types for us later on in our columns, rows, cells, etc. This is because the `data` type is literally defined as the `TData` generic type. Whatever you pass to the `data` table option will become the `TData` type for the rest of the table instance. Just make sure your column definitions use the same `TData` type as the `data` type when you define them later.\n\n```ts\n//note: data needs a \"stable\" reference to prevent wasteful re-computation (more on this below)\nconst data: Array<User> = []\n//or\nconst [data, setData] = React.useState<Array<User>>([])\n//or\nconst data = ref<Array<User>>([]) //vue\n//etc...\n```\n\n### Deep Keyed Data\n\nIf your data is not a nice flat array of objects, that's okay! Once you get around to defining your columns, there are strategies for accessing deeply nested data in your accessors.\n\nIf your `data` looks something like this:\n\n```json\n[\n  {\n    \"name\": {\n      \"first\": \"Tanner\",\n      \"last\": \"Linsley\"\n    },\n    \"info\": {\n      \"age\": 33,\n      \"visits\": 100\n    }\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": {\n      \"first\": \"Kevin\",\n      \"last\": \"Vandy\"\n    },\n    \"info\": {\n      \"age\": 27,\n      \"visits\": 200\n    }\n  }\n]\n```\n\nYou can define a type like this:\n\n```ts\ntype User = {\n  name: {\n    first: string\n    last: string\n  }\n  info: {\n    age: number\n    visits: number\n  }\n}\n```\n\nAnd you will be able to access the data in your column definitions with either dot notation in an accessorKey or simply by using an accessorFn.\n\n```ts\nconst columns = [\n  {\n    header: 'First Name',\n    accessorKey: 'name.first',\n  },\n  {\n    header: 'Last Name',\n    accessorKey: 'name.last',\n  },\n  {\n    header: 'Age',\n    accessorFn: (row) => row.info.age, //accessorFn receives the whole row object\n  },\n  //...\n]\n```\n\nThis is discussed in more detail in the [Column Def Guide](./column-defs).\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> The \"keys\" in your JSON data can usually be anything, but any periods in an `accessorKey` will be interpreted as a deep key path. If a key in your data contains a literal period, use an `accessorFn` to read it instead.\n\n### Nested Sub-Row Data\n\nIf you are using expanding features, it can be common to have nested sub-rows in your data. This results in a recursive type that is a bit different.\n\nSo if your data looks like this:\n\n```json\n[\n  {\n    \"firstName\": \"Tanner\",\n    \"lastName\": \"Linsley\",\n    \"subRows\": [\n      {\n        \"firstName\": \"Kevin\",\n        \"lastName\": \"Vandy\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"firstName\": \"John\",\n        \"lastName\": \"Doe\",\n        \"subRows\": []\n      }\n    ]\n  },\n  {\n    \"firstName\": \"Jane\",\n    \"lastName\": \"Doe\"\n  }\n]\n```\n\nYou can define a type like this:\n\n```ts\ntype User = {\n  firstName: string\n  lastName: string\n  subRows?: Array<User> //does not have to be called \"subRows\", can be called anything\n}\n```\n\nWhere `subRows` is an optional array of `User` objects. This is discussed in more detail in the [Expanding Guide](../framework/react/guide/expanding).\n\n### Data of Unknown Shape\n\nSometimes you cannot write a row type at all because the shape of the data is not known ahead of time (arbitrary API responses, user-uploaded CSV files, user-configurable reports). In that case, type your rows as a generic record:\n\n```ts\ntype DynamicRow = Record<string, unknown>\n\nconst data: Array<DynamicRow> = await fetchWhoKnowsWhat()\n```\n\nYou give up per-column value inference, but everything else still works. Column definitions are then generated from the data itself at runtime instead of being hard-coded. See [Dynamic Column Definitions](./column-defs#dynamic-column-definitions) in the Column Definitions Guide and the [Dynamic Columns example](../framework/react/examples/basic-dynamic-columns) (available for every framework adapter) for the full pattern, including runtime data type detection for sorting and filtering.\n\n## Give Data a \"Stable\" Reference\n\nThe `data` and `columns` arrays that you pass to the table instance should have \"stable\" references to prevent wasteful re-computation and rendering bugs.\n\nIn v9, table state lives in TanStack Store atoms, and the table instance is only created once. Passing a new `data` or `columns` reference on every render will not, by itself, throw the table into the classic v8-style infinite re-render loop. However, the row models and column structures are memoized against the `data` and `columns` references, so an unstable reference still has real consequences:\n\n- **Wasted re-computation.** A new `data` reference invalidates the core row model, so every row and cell object is rebuilt from scratch on every render (and any sorted, filtered, grouped, or paginated row models recompute right along with it). A new `columns` reference does the same for the column and header structures. For large datasets, this is a serious performance problem.\n- **Auto-reset render loops.** Features that automatically reset state when the rows are recalculated can still cause infinite re-render loops. For example, with client-side pagination enabled, `autoResetPageIndex` resets the page index whenever the row model recomputes. With an unstable `data` reference, that means a state update after every render, and each state update triggers another render with yet another new `data` reference.\n\nEither way, treat stable `data` and `columns` references as a requirement.\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> When React Compiler successfully compiles a component, it can provide stable identities for values and calculations automatically. TanStack Table's examples keep the explicit patterns below so they also work without the compiler. See the [React Compiler Guide](../framework/react/guide/react-compiler) for when manual memoization can be removed.\n\nHow you do this depends on which framework adapter you are using, but in React, you should often use `React.useState`, `React.useMemo`, or similar to ensure that both the `data` and `columns` table options have stable references.\n\n```tsx\nconst fallbackData: Array<User> = [] //✅ GOOD: stable empty fallback for when your data can be undefined (e.g. while a query loads)\nconst features = tableFeatures({}) //✅ GOOD: define outside the component for a stable reference\n\nexport default function MyComponent() {\n  //✅ GOOD: `columns` is a stable reference\n  const columns = useMemo(\n    () => [\n      // ...\n    ],\n    [],\n  )\n\n  //✅ GOOD: `data` is a stable reference\n  const [data, setData] = useState<Array<User>>(() => [\n    // ...\n  ])\n\n  // Columns and data have stable references, so the table only recomputes when they actually change\n  const table = useTable({\n    features,\n    columns,\n    data, // from useState, so always defined; use `data ?? fallbackData` when your data can be undefined\n  })\n\n  return <table>...</table>\n}\n```\n\n`React.useState` and `React.useMemo` are not the only ways to give your data a stable reference. You can also define your data outside of the component or use a 3rd party state management library like Redux, Zustand, or TanStack Query.\n\nThe main thing to avoid is defining the `data` array inside the same scope as the `useTable` call. That will cause the `data` array to be redefined on every render, which forces the table to rebuild every row on every render (and can loop forever when auto-reset features are involved).\n\n```tsx\nexport default function MyComponent() {\n  //❌ BAD: `features` is re-created on every render and re-applied to the table on every render\n  const features = tableFeatures({})\n\n  //😵 BAD: `columns` is redefined as a new array on every render, so the column and header structures are rebuilt on every render!\n  const columns = [\n    // ...\n  ]\n\n  //😵 BAD: `data` is redefined as a new array on every render, so every row is rebuilt on every render!\n  const data = [\n    // ...\n  ]\n\n  //❌ Columns and data are defined in the same scope as `useTable` without stable references\n  const table = useTable({\n    features,\n    columns,\n    data: data ?? [], //❌ Also bad because the fallback array is re-created on every render\n  })\n\n  return <table>...</table>\n}\n```\n\n### Memoize Data Transformations\n\nEven if your source data already has a stable reference, transforming it inline destroys that stability. Something as simple as an inline `data.filter()` creates a brand new array on every render.\n\n```tsx\nexport default function MyComponent() {\n  //✅ GOOD (TanStack Query provides stable references to data automatically)\n  const { data, isLoading } = useQuery({\n    //...\n  })\n\n  const table = useTable({\n    features,\n    columns,\n    //❌ BAD: This creates a new array on every render (destroys the stable reference)\n    data: data?.filter((d) => d.isActive) ?? fallbackData,\n  })\n\n  return <table>...</table>\n}\n```\n\nInstead, always memoize your data transformations. In React, this can be done with `useMemo` or similar.\n\n```tsx\nexport default function MyComponent() {\n  //✅ GOOD\n  const { data, isLoading } = useQuery({\n    //...\n  })\n\n  //✅ GOOD: `filteredData` only gets a new reference when `data` changes\n  const filteredData = useMemo(\n    () => data?.filter((d) => d.isActive) ?? [],\n    [data],\n  )\n\n  const table = useTable({\n    features,\n    columns,\n    data: filteredData, // stable reference!\n  })\n\n  return <table>...</table>\n}\n```\n\nSee the [With TanStack Query example](../framework/react/examples/with-tanstack-query) for a complete integration with fetched data.\n\n## How TanStack Table Transforms Data\n\nLater, in other parts of these docs, you will see how TanStack Table processes the `data` that you pass to the table and generates the row and cell objects that are used to create the table. The `data` that you pass to the table is never mutated by TanStack Table, but the actual values in the rows and cells may be transformed by the accessors in your column definitions, or by other transformations performed by [row models](./row-models) like grouping or aggregation.\n\n## How Much Data Can TanStack Table Handle?\n\nTanStack Table's client-side row models are built to handle large datasets, and the official examples include much larger stress tests. The practical limit depends on the payload, table features, rendering strategy, and target hardware, so test with representative data instead of choosing from row count alone.\n\nSee the [Client-Side vs Server-Side Guide](./client-side-vs-server-side) for the complete decision framework and the distinction between data processing, pagination, and virtualization.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: Data Guide\n---\n\nTables start with your data. Your column definitions and rows will depend on the shape of your data. TanStack Table has some TypeScript features that will help you create the rest of your table code with a great type-safe experience. If you set up your data and types correctly, TanStack Table will be able to infer the shape of your data and enforce that your column definitions are made correctly.\n\n## TypeScript\n\nTypeScript is NOT required to use the TanStack Table packages... **_BUT_** TanStack Table is written and organized in such a way that makes the awesome TypeScript experience that you get feel like it is one of the main selling points of the library. If you are not using TypeScript, you will be missing out on a lot of great autocompletion and type-checking features that will both speed up your development time and reduce the number of bugs in your code.\n\n### TypeScript Generics\n\nHaving a basic understanding of what TypeScript Generics are and how they work will help you understand this guide better, but it should be easy enough to pick up as you go. The official [TypeScript Generics Docs](https://www.typescriptlang.org/docs/handbook/2/generics.html) may be helpful for those not yet familiar with TypeScript.\n\n### Defining Data Types\n\n`data` is an array of objects that will be turned into the rows of your table. Each object in the array represents a row of data (under normal circumstances). If you are using TypeScript, we usually define a type for the shape of our data. This type is used as a generic type for all of the other table, column, row, and cell instances. This generic is usually referred to as `TData` throughout the rest of the TanStack Table types and APIs.\n\nFor example, if we have a table that displays a list of users in an array like this:\n\n```json\n[\n  {\n    \"firstName\": \"Tanner\",\n    \"lastName\": \"Linsley\",\n    \"age\": 33,\n    \"visits\": 100,\n    \"progress\": 50,\n    \"status\": \"Married\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"firstName\": \"Kevin\",\n    \"lastName\": \"Vandy\",\n    \"age\": 27,\n    \"visits\": 200,\n    \"progress\": 100,\n    \"status\": \"Single\"\n  }\n]\n```\n\nThen we can define a User (TData) type like this:\n\n```ts\n//TData\ntype User = {\n  firstName: string\n  lastName: string\n  age: number\n  visits: number\n  progress: number\n  status: string\n}\n```\n\nWe can then define our `data` array with this type, and then TanStack Table will be able to intelligently infer lots of types for us later on in our columns, rows, cells, etc. This is because the `data` type is literally defined as the `TData` generic type. Whatever you pass to the `data` table option will become the `TData` type for the rest of the table instance. Just make sure your column definitions use the same `TData` type as the `data` type when you define them later.\n\n```ts\n//note: data needs a \"stable\" reference to prevent wasteful re-computation (more on this below)\nconst data: Array<User> = []\n//or\nconst [data, setData] = React.useState<Array<User>>([])\n//or\nconst data = ref<Array<User>>([]) //vue\n//etc...\n```\n\n### Deep Keyed Data\n\nIf your data is not a nice flat array of objects, that's okay! Once you get around to defining your columns, there are strategies for accessing deeply nested data in your accessors.\n\nIf your `data` looks something like this:\n\n```json\n[\n  {\n    \"name\": {\n      \"first\": \"Tanner\",\n      \"last\": \"Linsley\"\n    },\n    \"info\": {\n      \"age\": 33,\n      \"visits\": 100\n    }\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": {\n      \"first\": \"Kevin\",\n      \"last\": \"Vandy\"\n    },\n    \"info\": {\n      \"age\": 27,\n      \"visits\": 200\n    }\n  }\n]\n```\n\nYou can define a type like this:\n\n```ts\ntype User = {\n  name: {\n    first: string\n    last: string\n  }\n  info: {\n    age: number\n    visits: number\n  }\n}\n```\n\nAnd you will be able to access the data in your column definitions with either dot notation in an accessorKey or simply by using an accessorFn.\n\n```ts\nconst columns = [\n  {\n    header: 'First Name',\n    accessorKey: 'name.first',\n  },\n  {\n    header: 'Last Name',\n    accessorKey: 'name.last',\n  },\n  {\n    header: 'Age',\n    accessorFn: (row) => row.info.age, //accessorFn receives the whole row object\n  },\n  //...\n]\n```\n\nThis is discussed in more detail in the [Column Def Guide](./column-defs).\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> The \"keys\" in your JSON data can usually be anything, but any periods in an `accessorKey` will be interpreted as a deep key path. If a key in your data contains a literal period, use an `accessorFn` to read it instead.\n\n### Nested Sub-Row Data\n\nIf you are using expanding features, it can be common to have nested sub-rows in your data. This results in a recursive type that is a bit different.\n\nSo if your data looks like this:\n\n```json\n[\n  {\n    \"firstName\": \"Tanner\",\n    \"lastName\": \"Linsley\",\n    \"subRows\": [\n      {\n        \"firstName\": \"Kevin\",\n        \"lastName\": \"Vandy\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"firstName\": \"John\",\n        \"lastName\": \"Doe\",\n        \"subRows\": []\n      }\n    ]\n  },\n  {\n    \"firstName\": \"Jane\",\n    \"lastName\": \"Doe\"\n  }\n]\n```\n\nYou can define a type like this:\n\n```ts\ntype User = {\n  firstName: string\n  lastName: string\n  subRows?: Array<User> //does not have to be called \"subRows\", can be called anything\n}\n```\n\nWhere `subRows` is an optional array of `User` objects. This is discussed in more detail in the [Expanding Guide](../framework/react/guide/expanding).\n\n### Data of Unknown Shape\n\nSometimes you cannot write a row type at all because the shape of the data is not known ahead of time (arbitrary API responses, user-uploaded CSV files, user-configurable reports). In that case, type your rows as a generic record:\n\n```ts\ntype DynamicRow = Record<string, unknown>\n\nconst data: Array<DynamicRow> = await fetchWhoKnowsWhat()\n```\n\nYou give up per-column value inference, but everything else still works. Column definitions are then generated from the data itself at runtime instead of being hard-coded. See [Dynamic Column Definitions](./column-defs#dynamic-column-definitions) in the Column Definitions Guide and the [Dynamic Columns example](../framework/react/examples/basic-dynamic-columns) (available for every framework adapter) for the full pattern, including runtime data type detection for sorting and filtering.\n\n## Give Data a \"Stable\" Reference\n\nThe `data` and `columns` arrays that you pass to the table instance should have \"stable\" references to prevent wasteful re-computation and rendering bugs.\n\nIn v9, table state lives in TanStack Store atoms, and the table instance is only created once. Passing a new `data` or `columns` reference on every render will not, by itself, throw the table into the classic v8-style infinite re-render loop. However, the row models and column structures are memoized against the `data` and `columns` references, so an unstable reference still has real consequences:\n\n- **Wasted re-computation.** A new `data` reference invalidates the core row model, so every row and cell object is rebuilt from scratch on every render (and any sorted, filtered, grouped, or paginated row models recompute right along with it). A new `columns` reference does the same for the column and header structures. For large datasets, this is a serious performance problem.\n- **Auto-reset render loops.** Features that automatically reset state when the rows are recalculated can still cause infinite re-render loops. For example, with client-side pagination enabled, `autoResetPageIndex` resets the page index whenever the row model recomputes. With an unstable `data` reference, that means a state update after every render, and each state update triggers another render with yet another new `data` reference.\n\nEither way, treat stable `data` and `columns` references as a requirement.\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> When React Compiler successfully compiles a component, it can provide stable identities for values and calculations automatically. TanStack Table's examples keep the explicit patterns below so they also work without the compiler. See the [React Compiler Guide](../framework/react/guide/react-compiler) for when manual memoization can be removed.\n\nHow you do this depends on which framework adapter you are using, but in React, you should often use `React.useState`, `React.useMemo`, or similar to ensure that both the `data` and `columns` table options have stable references.\n\n```tsx\nconst fallbackData: Array<User> = [] //✅ GOOD: stable empty fallback for when your data can be undefined (e.g. while a query loads)\nconst features = tableFeatures({}) //✅ GOOD: define outside the component for a stable reference\n\nexport default function MyComponent() {\n  //✅ GOOD: `columns` is a stable reference\n  const columns = useMemo(\n    () => [\n      // ...\n    ],\n    [],\n  )\n\n  //✅ GOOD: `data` is a stable reference\n  const [data, setData] = useState<Array<User>>(() => [\n    // ...\n  ])\n\n  // Columns and data have stable references, so the table only recomputes when they actually change\n  const table = useTable({\n    features,\n    columns,\n    data, // from useState, so always defined; use `data ?? fallbackData` when your data can be undefined\n  })\n\n  return <table>...</table>\n}\n```\n\n`React.useState` and `React.useMemo` are not the only ways to give your data a stable reference. You can also define your data outside of the component or use a 3rd party state management library like Redux, Zustand, or TanStack Query.\n\nThe main thing to avoid is defining the `data` array inside the same scope as the `useTable` call. That will cause the `data` array to be redefined on every render, which forces the table to rebuild every row on every render (and can loop forever when auto-reset features are involved).\n\n```tsx\nexport default function MyComponent() {\n  //❌ BAD: `features` is re-created on every render and re-applied to the table on every render\n  const features = tableFeatures({})\n\n  //😵 BAD: `columns` is redefined as a new array on every render, so the column and header structures are rebuilt on every render!\n  const columns = [\n    // ...\n  ]\n\n  //😵 BAD: `data` is redefined as a new array on every render, so every row is rebuilt on every render!\n  const data = [\n    // ...\n  ]\n\n  //❌ Columns and data are defined in the same scope as `useTable` without stable references\n  const table = useTable({\n    features,\n    columns,\n    data: data ?? [], //❌ Also bad because the fallback array is re-created on every render\n  })\n\n  return <table>...</table>\n}\n```\n\n### Memoize Data Transformations\n\nEven if your source data already has a stable reference, transforming it inline destroys that stability. Something as simple as an inline `data.filter()` creates a brand new array on every render.\n\n```tsx\nexport default function MyComponent() {\n  //✅ GOOD (TanStack Query provides stable references to data automatically)\n  const { data, isLoading } = useQuery({\n    //...\n  })\n\n  const table = useTable({\n    features,\n    columns,\n    //❌ BAD: This creates a new array on every render (destroys the stable reference)\n    data: data?.filter((d) => d.isActive) ?? fallbackData,\n  })\n\n  return <table>...</table>\n}\n```\n\nInstead, always memoize your data transformations. In React, this can be done with `useMemo` or similar.\n\n```tsx\nexport default function MyComponent() {\n  //✅ GOOD\n  const { data, isLoading } = useQuery({\n    //...\n  })\n\n  //✅ GOOD: `filteredData` only gets a new reference when `data` changes\n  const filteredData = useMemo(\n    () => data?.filter((d) => d.isActive) ?? [],\n    [data],\n  )\n\n  const table = useTable({\n    features,\n    columns,\n    data: filteredData, // stable reference!\n  })\n\n  return <table>...</table>\n}\n```\n\nSee the [With TanStack Query example](../framework/react/examples/with-tanstack-query) for a complete integration with fetched data.\n\n## How TanStack Table Transforms Data\n\nLater, in other parts of these docs, you will see how TanStack Table processes the `data` that you pass to the table and generates the row and cell objects that are used to create the table. The `data` that you pass to the table is never mutated by TanStack Table, but the actual values in the rows and cells may be transformed by the accessors in your column definitions, or by other transformations performed by [row models](./row-models) like grouping or aggregation.\n\n## How Much Data Can TanStack Table Handle?\n\nTanStack Table's client-side row models are built to handle large datasets, and the official examples include much larger stress tests. The practical limit depends on the payload, table features, rendering strategy, and target hardware, so test with representative data instead of choosing from row count alone.\n\nSee the [Client-Side vs Server-Side Guide](./client-side-vs-server-side) for the complete decision framework and the distinction between data processing, pagination, and virtualization.\n"}
